"10 from 3 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Single elimination tournament."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This class produced the best boxing of the day, with three Frenchman and the Australian " [:a {:href "/athletes/45153"} "Reginald “Snowy” Baker"] " contesting the title against six British boxers. The last four ABA Champions were present - " [:a {:href "/athletes/3389"} "John Douglas"] " (1905), " [:a {:href "/athletes/3462"} "Arthur Murdoch"] " (1906), " [:a {:href "/athletes/3519"} "Rube Warnes"] " (1907 and 1910), and " [:a {:href "/athletes/3373"} "Bill Child"] " (1908-09 and 1911)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final came down to Douglas and Baker, and was described as the best match of the tournament. The first round was even. Douglas knocked Baker down in round two, but he continued in a closely fought battle. However, Douglas hung on in round three to win on points. Douglas was presented his gold medal by his father, who was then the President of the ABA. John Douglas, the son, was actually better known as a cricketer, and later captained Essex County and England teams. He and his father died tragically in a sea disaster returning from Finland, in December 1930."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Snowy Baker is the greatest all-around athlete ever produced by Australia. He competed at the 1908 Olympics in boxing, " [:a {:href "/results/54013"} "diving"] ", and swimming. In addition to those three sports, he represented Australia at the international level in rugby union and water polo. He was also state or national caliber in cricket, track &amp; field athletics, and rowing. In December 1908, he was appointed referee for the Jack Johnson vs. Tommy Burns heavyweight professional bout, which was held in Sydney, but Johnson objected to Baker’s snow-white hair (hence his nickname), and he was replaced. Baker later became a movie star and stuntman."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor